# English style (Elementplan)

English is a translation target, not the source. Write from the German meaning,
never from German word order. A sentence that a British project manager would
not say out loud is wrong, even when every word is a correct dictionary match.

## Spelling: Oxford British English

Same convention as ISO 19650: British spelling, but `-ize` / `-ization` endings.

| Use | Not | Note |
|---|---|---|
| modelling, modelled, modeller | modeling, modeled | also remodelling |
| storey, storeys | story, stories | `story` only for narrative |
| colour, behaviour, favour, neighbour, labour | color, behavior, … | |
| centre, metre, metres, fibre, litre | center, meter, … | `meter` stays for metering devices: gas meter, water meter |
| catalogue, dialogue, analogue | catalog, dialog | |
| programme (room programme, functional programme) | program | `program` only for software |
| fulfil, fulfils, fulfilment | fulfill, fulfillment | |
| cancelled, levelled, signalling, travelling | canceled, leveled | double the final `l` |
| grey, kerb, ageing, judgement, licence (noun) | gray, curb, aging | `license` stays the verb |
| organization, optimize, visualization, prioritize, harmonize, standardize, normalize, synchronize | organisation, optimise, visualisation, … | `-ize`, not `-ise` |
| analysis, analyse | analyzis | noun `analysis`, verb `analyse` |

## Terminology

| German | English | Never |
|---|---|---|
| Bauherr, Bauherrschaft, Auftraggeber | client | owner, employer, building owner, principal |
| Planer, Fachplaner, Planung (die Rolle) | designer, specialist designer | planner — a false friend; an English planner does programming or town planning |
| Bestand, Bestandsbau | existing building, existing structure | existing stock, stock |
| Fachbereich, Fachdisziplin | discipline | domain, subject area |
| Gewerk, Unternehmerlos | trade, contractor package | craft, guild |
| Vergabepaket | procurement package | award lot, tender lot |
| Planungsschnittstelle | planning interface | design cut |
| Geschoss | storey | floor (ambiguous with floor slab) |
| Raumprogramm | room programme | room program, space program |
| Nutzungsvereinbarung | usage agreement | utilisation agreement |
| Plausibilisierung | plausibility check | plausibilisation |
| fachlich beurteilen | get a specialist assessment | assess professionally |
| Plangrafik | drawing graphics | plan graphics |
| Erfüllungsgrad | how far the order is met | degree of fulfilment |
| Bauteil | component | building part, construction element |
| Anwendungsfall | use case | application case |
| Werteliste | value list | list of values |
| Abnahme | handover, acceptance | takeover |
| Rückbau | deconstruction | dismantling (unless literal) |
| Terrain | terrain | ground level (when the attribute is BelowTerrain) |

`Elementplan`, `pragmaticBIM`, `abstractBIM`, `Madaster` and IFC identifiers
stay untranslated. `pragmaticBIM-Referenz` is *pragmaticBIM Reference*, short
*the Reference* (capitalized). `Webanwendung` is *web application*; see
`.cursor/rules/naming.mdc` for which subject does what.

## Register

1. Write what happens, not what is theoretically possible. `The model shows …`,
   not `The model offers the possibility of showing …`.
2. Prefer a verb over a noun chain. `decide early` beats `early decision-making`.
3. Drop `as well as` when `and` works, and `in each case` / `respectively` /
   `thereby` / `hereby` entirely – they are German connectives in disguise.
4. Keep the German sentence count. Do not merge two German sentences into one
   English one, and do not split one into two; the book layout depends on length.
5. Enumerations after an em dash stay lower case: `` `NEW` — new ``.

## Dashes and numbers (Swiss)

Use dashes sparingly.

| Role | Form | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Parenthetical aside | spaced en dash ` – ` | `… delivers – in every role.` |
| Token / definition gloss | em dash `—` after backtick or bold label | `` `NEW` — new ``, `**Concept** — …` |
| Compound | hyphen `-` | `timber-concrete` |

Numbers follow Swiss convention in all languages: thousands with apostrophe
(`1'000`), decimal point (`3.14`), space before percent (`80 %`, `± 25 %`).

## Structure is fixed

Section headers translate one-to-one and nothing else moves:

| de | en |
|---|---|
| Beschreibung | Description |
| Modellierungshinweis | Modelling guidelines |
| Zulässige Werte | Allowed values |
| Hinweis | Note |
| Beispiel | Example |

IFC entities, property set names, enum tokens in backticks, FEEL expressions,
and `pragmaticbim:` identifiers are never translated. Norm references keep their
original designation: `SIA 416`, `SN 506 511`, `ÖNORM A 2063`, `VeVA`.

One deliberate exception: free-text `ObjectType` and `LongName` values are
localized per language, and `applicability` follows them. `ObjectType=Humus` in
`de` is `ObjectType=Topsoil` in `en` because `values/objecttype-humus.yaml`
carries a separate `values:` array per language. Do not "correct" these to
match across languages.

```yaml
# ❌ BAD — calque, -ise ending, wrong client term
en: |-
  Identifies if the component lies under terrain, whereby the building owner
  can optimise the existing stock as well as the room program.

# ✅ GOOD
en: |-
  Marks whether the component is below terrain, so the client can optimize the
  existing building and the room programme.
```
